---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { shapes } from '../../data/shapes';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'Face shape guides',
  description:
    'What each of the six face shapes is, how to check it with a ruler, and which hairstyles and glasses frames work with it.',
  hasPart: shapes.map((shape) => ({
    '@type': 'Article',
    name: shape.h1,
    url: `/shapes/${shape.slug}/`,
    description: shape.description,
  })),
};

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How many face shapes are there?',
    a: 'Most charts use six or seven categories. Six — oval, round, square, heart, oblong and diamond — cover the proportions this tool measures. Some charts add a seventh, triangle, for a jaw that is wider than the forehead.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you have two face shapes?',
    a: 'Yes, and many people do. The categories are regions on a continuous space of proportions, not distinct boxes, so a face can sit between oval and oblong, or between square and round. That is why this tool always shows the runner-up shape alongside the result.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does face shape determine what looks good on you?',
    a: 'It is one input, not a verdict. Face shape tells you where a hairstyle or a frame will add width, and where it will add length. Expression, grooming, lighting and camera angle all change how a face reads, often more than the underlying proportions do.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I measure my face shape without a photo?',
    a: 'Stand in front of a mirror with a tape measure. Measure hairline to chin (L), across your cheekbones (W), your forehead (F) and your jaw (J). The ratio L÷W sorts long faces from compact ones, and comparing F with J and W tells you where your face is widest.',
  },
];

const jsonLdFaq = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Face Shape Guides — The Six Shapes Explained"
  description="Plain-English guides to the six face shapes: the proportions that define each one, how to check it with a tape measure, and what suits it."
  jsonLd={[jsonLd, jsonLdFaq]}
>
  <h1>Face shape guides</h1>

  <p>
    Face shape is a description of bone-structure proportions. It is useful for one specific job:
    knowing where a hairstyle, a pair of glasses or a beard will add width, and where it will add
    length. It is not a ranking, and it does not describe how anyone looks.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each guide below covers the proportions that define the shape, how to check it with a tape
    measure, and what to do with the answer. If you would rather not measure anything, the
    <a href="/">face shape detector</a> does the measuring in your browser.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the six shapes differ</h2>

  <p>
    Every shape comes down to two questions: how long the face is relative to its width, and which
    of the three widths — forehead, cheekbones, jaw — is the largest.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Shape</th>
        <th>Length ÷ width</th>
        <th>Widest point</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        shapes.map((shape) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <a href={`/shapes/${shape.slug}/`}>{shape.name}</a>
            </td>
            <td>{shape.rule}</td>
            <td>{shape.signs[0]}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The guides</h2>

  <ul>
    {
      shapes.map((shape) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`/shapes/${shape.slug}/`}>
            <strong>{shape.h1}</strong>
          </a>{' '}
          — {shape.summary}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>

  {
    faqs.map((item) => (
      <section>
        <h3>{item.q}</h3>
        <p>{item.a}</p>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <p class="disclaimer">
    These guides describe proportions and styling, not attractiveness. If a shape is listed here as
    "avoid", it means it works against the geometry — not that it looks bad.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
